## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible components using the Strategy pattern with a central registry, a per-flag isolation policy, and minimal, type-safe interfaces. It also touches on runtime extensibility and observability trade-offs relevant at Apple/NVIDIA scale.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Interchangeable evaluators that implement a common interface, allowing new strategies to be added without modifying existing code
- **Registry Pattern**: Dynamic lookup by name enables runtime registration without rebuilding the application
- **Per-flag Isolation**: Each flag maintains its own context and state, preventing cross-flag contamination
- **Observability**: Built-in hooks for metrics collection and logging

## Design Pattern: Strategy + Registry

The Strategy pattern is the primary choice here because it encapsulates each evaluation algorithm (Boolean, PercentageOfTime, ABTest) behind a common interface, making them interchangeable. Combined with a Registry, this enables runtime extensibility—new strategies can be registered by name and immediately available for use. This combination provides:

- **Open/Closed Principle**: Open for new strategies, closed for modification
- **Runtime flexibility**: Add evaluators without redeployment
- **Type safety**: TypeScript interfaces ensure contracts are enforced

## Code Example

```typescript
interface FlagContext {
  userId?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

interface IFlagEvaluator {
  readonly name: string;
  evaluate(flagKey: string, context: FlagContext): boolean;
}

class BooleanEvaluator implements IFlagEvaluator {
  readonly name = 'boolean';
  evaluate(flagKey: string, context: FlagContext): boolean {
    return true; // or false based on flag configuration
  }
}

class PercentageOfTimeEvaluator implements IFlagEvaluator {
  readonly name = 'percentageOfTime';

  private readonly hash: (userId: string, flagKey: string) => number;

  constructor(private percentage: number) {
    this.hash = (userId: string, flagKey: string): number => {
      let hash = 0;
      const str = `${userId}:${flagKey}`;
      for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
        hash = ((hash << 5) - hash) + str.charCodeAt(i);
        hash |= 0;
      }
      return Math.abs(hash) % 100;
    };
  }

  evaluate(flagKey: string, context: FlagContext): boolean {
    const userId = context.userId ?? 'anonymous';
    return this.hash(userId, flagKey) < this.percentage;
  }
}

class FlagEngine {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();
  private flagOverrides = new Map<string, string>();
  private metrics: Array<{ flag: string; evaluator: string; result: boolean }> = [];

  register(evaluator: IFlagEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator);
  }

  setFlagStrategy(flagKey: string, evaluatorName: string, config?: unknown): void {
    if (!this.evaluators.has(evaluatorName)) {
      throw new Error(`Evaluator '${evaluatorName}' not registered`);
    }
    this.flagOverrides.set(flagKey, evaluatorName);
  }

  isEnabled(flagKey: string, context: FlagContext = {}): boolean {
    const evaluatorName = this.flagOverrides.get(flagKey) ?? 'boolean';
    const evaluator = this.evaluators.get(evaluatorName)!;

    const result = evaluator.evaluate(flagKey, context);

    this.metrics.push({ flag: flagKey, evaluator: evaluatorName, result });

    return result;
  }

  getMetrics() {
    return [...this.metrics];
  }
}

// Usage
const engine = new FlagEngine();
engine.register(new BooleanEvaluator());
engine.register(new PercentageOfTimeEvaluator(25)); // 25% of users

engine.setFlagStrategy('new-dashboard', 'percentageOfTime', { percentage: 25 });

console.log(engine.isEnabled('new-dashboard', { userId: 'user-123' }));
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes mastery of runtime extensibility, safe composition of hot-pluggable components, and streaming guarantees in a real-world UI analytics context.

## Key Concepts

